import "core"


# A data type eighteen fields wide, declared, shown, and printed, which is what holds that a type
  costs what its width says it costs.
  Typechecking does not move with the width at all. This file is 9 epochs over 3 fragments, and so
  is the same shape at twenty-four fields, at thirty-two and at forty-eight. Measured with
  @catln epochs@ against @io.ct@, which declares nothing, the whole compilation is 799 epochs where
  that baseline is 793: six epochs for a type eighteen fields wide. A page is partitioned into solve
  fragments per function SCC, so a declaration this wide is its own small component rather than
  something the rest of the page is solved against.
  Building the arrow trees for it is where a width still costs, and only for a value something
  READS. Against a 54-second floor:
    18 fields, printed     — 54s
    20 fields, printed     — 70s
    22 fields, printed     — 104s
    24 fields, printed     — 245s
    24 fields, NOT printed — 58s
  Past twenty-four it flattens rather than running away — 32 fields is 346s and 48 is 306s, both
  held at about half a core, which is a build spending its time in memory rather than in work. So
  the climb sits between eighteen and twenty-four and there is a ceiling above it.
  That is why the value below is printed and not only declared. Without the @>@ the program
  typechecks, builds nothing for the value, and this file would pass while saying nothing about the
  half of the cost that is real.
  Eighteen and not a round number, because that is what a checkbox is. A note in a vault running the
  Obsidian Tasks plugin carries a status, a description, tags, a priority, six dates, a recurrence,
  an action on completion, an id, the ids it waits on and a block link — eighteen things, which is
  the width below. [/Data/Note/Task/Obsidian] is the type such a checkbox is read into.
data(Wide(a -> String, b -> String, c -> String, d -> String, e -> String, f -> String, g -> String, h -> String, i -> String, j -> String, k -> String, l -> String, m -> String, n -> String, o -> String, p -> String, q -> String, r -> String))

every(Wide.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

/Wide(a= "1", b= "2", c= "3", d= "4", e= "5", f= "6", g= "7", h= "8", i= "9", j= "10", k= "11", l= "12", m= "13", n= "14", o= "15", p= "16", q= "17", r= "18")

# Printed so that the value is built and not only declared, as @valueDeclFields.ct@ does and for the
  same reason: the width that costs anything is the width something reads.
> /Wide(a= "1", b= "2", c= "3", d= "4", e= "5", f= "6", g= "7", h= "8", i= "9", j= "10", k= "11", l= "12", m= "13", n= "14", o= "15", p= "16", q= "17", r= "18")
main{io -> IO}= io.exit(val= 0)
